#!/bin/sh
# Shutdown: persist entropy, flush, unmount. Busybox init runs this for
# both poweroff and reboot.
/bin/dd if=/dev/urandom of=/data/random-seed bs=512 count=1 2>/dev/null
/bin/sync

# Power off at the PMIC rather than returning to init for reboot(RB_POWER_OFF).
# That path does not stay off while VBUS is present: the device is back in about
# seven seconds reporting bootreason=charger, which turns an idle handheld on a
# charger into a boot loop as soon as the frontend's doze timeout fires. Writing
# the PMU's own soft-poweroff bit does stay off — measured on an RG SP, from
# button and charger boots alike.
#
# Only on a poweroff. BusyBox init runs this single `::shutdown:` action for
# reboot as well and gives no way to tell them apart, so baseos-poweroff leaves
# a marker and baseos-reboot clears one. Absent the marker we take the reboot
# path on purpose: the two failures are not symmetric. A poweroff that falls
# through merely restarts on the charger — the behaviour this OS already
# shipped — while a reboot that powers off strands baseos-update after a slot
# flip, leaving the device sitting there off mid-update with no boot counted at
# all.
#
# The unarmed probe is the third guard. `axp-off` with no --now writes nothing
# and succeeds only after it has found the driver's client, matched both the
# address and the part name it publishes, opened /dev/i2c-N and read REG27H —
# an exact "can this work on this board" test. One AXP part has been measured,
# on an RG SP, and the allowlist holds only that one; on any board where that
# probe fails this script behaves exactly as it did before the PMIC write
# existed.
#
# What then runs, in order, and what each step is worth:
#
#   * The unmounts, by name rather than `umount -a`: that would take devtmpfs
#     with it and the i2c character device has to outlive this step.
#
#     -r is load-bearing here. BusyBox init runs the `::shutdown:` action
#     *before* its kill(-1, SIGTERM) sweep, so the frontend is still running —
#     a shell with its cwd on the card, reading its launch.sh from it — and
#     bluetooth daemons may still hold /data/bluetooth. Both unmounts therefore
#     return EBUSY on an ordinary poweroff from a frontend. Without -r that
#     would leave a writable vfat/exfat mount live when the rails drop; with it
#     they usually degrade to a read-only remount instead, which is a safe
#     state to cut power in. Usually, not always: a busy cwd is what -r turns
#     read-only, but a file still open for writing refuses the remount the same
#     way it refused the unmount, and the mount stays rw. That gap is part of
#     why quiescing the frontend first is a separate change. Assume degraded,
#     not unmounted.
#   * axp-off --now last, because nothing downstream of a cut that lands runs at
#     all. It returns whenever the rails have not dropped *yet* — a write that
#     failed, or one whose effect has not landed — and then the umount below
#     runs and init is handed back exactly as this script always did.
if [ -e /run/poweroff-requested ] && [ -x /usr/sbin/axp-off ] \
	&& /usr/sbin/axp-off >/dev/null 2>&1; then
	/bin/umount -r /mnt/sdcard 2>/dev/null
	/bin/umount -r /data 2>/dev/null
	/bin/sync
	/usr/sbin/axp-off --now
fi

/bin/umount -a -r 2>/dev/null
